---
import CaseStudy from "../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro";
import Block from "../../components/Block.astro";
import { projects } from "../../lib/projects";

const project = projects.find((p) => p.slug === "clearsky")!;
---

<CaseStudy
  project={project}
  kicker="serverless · 2026"
  lede="Answer two questions for any AWS account, continuously: what is running, and is it safe? Serverless end to end — it stands up or tears down in a single pipeline run."
>
  <Block label="problem" heading="Posture tools tell you what is wrong, then ask you to trust them">
    <p>
      Security scanners are usually opaque: a score appears and the reasoning behind it
      belongs to someone else. I wanted findings that were reproducible — the same account
      state producing the same findings every time — with any AI strictly additive, able to
      explain and investigate but never to decide what counts as a finding.
    </p>
    <p>
      The second constraint was cost. This runs in a personal AWS account, so nothing may
      idle: no always-on compute, no NAT gateway, no managed scanner service, and the whole
      platform tears down when it is not in use.
    </p>
  </Block>

  <Block label="architecture" heading="Serverless, and read-only by construction">
    <figure class="arch">
      <img
        src={project.image}
        alt={project.imageAlt}
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption>
        A private S3 bucket behind CloudFront with origin access control serves a multipage
        dashboard; one Lambda behind a function URL handles the API; DynamoDB holds the
        finding lifecycle; member accounts are reached by cross-account assume-role.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      The detectors are pure functions — API responses in, findings out — so they are tested
      against fixtures rather than mocked against live AWS. The whole backend runs on the
      Python standard library plus boto3: no layers, no third-party packages, nothing to
      patch. JWT verification, RS256 included, is implemented in-process against Cognito's
      JWKS.
    </p>
    <p>
      Login is a custom page rather than Cognito's hosted UI, authenticating with
      <code>USER_PASSWORD_AUTH</code> over fetch, which is what makes the new-password,
      forgot-password and silent-refresh flows behave the way the rest of the dashboard does.
    </p>
  </Block>

  <Block label="decisions and tradeoffs" heading="What I chose, and what it cost">
    <dl class="rows">
      <div class="row">
        <dt>A Lambda function URL instead of API Gateway</dt>
        <dd>
          One less service to run and pay for. The tradeoff is real: no usage plans and no
          request validation, so validation lives in the handler.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div class="row">
        <dt>Detectors as pure functions</dt>
        <dd>
          Deterministic and fixture-testable, which is what makes a finding trustworthy. The
          cost is that every new AWS API shape needs a fixture before it needs a detector.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div class="row">
        <dt>Read-only IAM as the AI's hard boundary</dt>
        <dd>
          The agent's tool loop is allowlisted to describe, list and get calls. It cannot
          mutate the account, so a prompt injection cannot become an incident — the boundary
          is IAM, not a system prompt.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div class="row">
        <dt>A zero-dependency backend</dt>
        <dd>
          No supply chain to audit and no layer builds in CI. The cost is code written by
          hand that a library would otherwise provide, including RS256 verification.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div class="row">
        <dt>Cost Explorer results cached per preset per day</dt>
        <dd>
          Cost Explorer charges per query. Caching keeps a page that is all about cost from
          being the expensive part of the platform.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </Block>

  <Block label="what broke" heading="A login loop, and an origin that refused to be verified">
    <p>
      <strong>CloudFront OAC to a Lambda function URL never worked.</strong> With SigV4
      signing configured, the origin returned 403 and the function was never invoked —
      correct OAC config, correct permissions, correct origin request policy. I could not
      find a root cause and stopped trying to. The replacement is a secret header injected by
      CloudFront and checked as the first thing the handler does, with the JWT still gating
      every route. Two weaker mechanisms composed, instead of one that would not work.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two related traps showed up on the way: the browser reserves the
      <code>Authorization</code> header for origin auth when OAC is enabled, so the token
      moved to <code>x-authorization</code>; and <code>x-amz-content-sha256</code> cannot be
      whitelisted in an origin request policy at all, because it is reserved.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Then production ate itself.</strong> The API client signed the user out on
      <em>any</em> 401 or 403. The origin-verify path — the newly added, least-tested path —
      returned 403, so every request bounced the user to the login page, which made a
      request, which returned 403. An infinite loop out of two individually reasonable
      decisions.
    </p>
    <p>
      The fix was to stop treating authentication and authorisation failures as the same
      thing: only a 401 ends the session, and only after one silent refresh has been tried; a
      403 raises a message and leaves the session alone. That distinction is now the first
      thing I check in any client I write.
    </p>
  </Block>

  <Block label="numbers" heading="What it covered, and what it left behind">
    <ul class="metrics">
      <li><b>24+</b><span>read-only detectors, every enabled region</span></li>
      <li><b>113</b><span>unit tests against fixtures</span></li>
      <li><b>66</b><span>findings on the first real scan (35 high, 9 medium, 22 low)</span></li>
      <li><b>none</b><span>left running — destroyed from its own pipeline</span></li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      It redeploys from an empty account by pushing to <code>main</code>. That is the part I
      care about most: the platform is not a thing that exists on a server somewhere, it is a
      description that can be re-created on demand.
    </p>
  </Block>
</CaseStudy>
